WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.000
So in 2026, we're basically trusting equations to decide, you know, pretty much everything.

00:06.140 --> 00:06.580
Oh, absolutely.

00:06.800 --> 00:06.940
Right.

00:07.020 --> 00:07.280
Everything.

00:07.700 --> 00:10.500
I mean, from which global charities get billions of dollars in funding

00:10.500 --> 00:16.280
to whether an artificial intelligence model is just deemed too dangerous to turn on.

00:16.420 --> 00:19.260
Yeah, we really just look at the spreadsheets and the algorithms and we,

00:19.780 --> 00:21.700
well, we think the math is completely objective.

00:22.100 --> 00:22.480
Exactly.

00:22.660 --> 00:27.300
We just type in the parameters and we trust the output almost blindly, really.

00:27.300 --> 00:31.180
And, you know, that is sort of the terrifying thing about optimization

00:31.180 --> 00:34.420
because the math always does exactly what you tell it to do.

00:34.860 --> 00:37.540
The danger isn't necessarily that the calculations are wrong.

00:38.040 --> 00:43.880
The danger is that the structural equations we use to define, like, goodness or safety,

00:44.440 --> 00:47.420
they possess these massive invisible trapdoors.

00:48.040 --> 00:51.820
Trapdoors that we just fall right through before we even hit the enter key.

00:52.100 --> 00:52.500
Exactly.

00:52.880 --> 00:54.400
So welcome to the Deep Dive.

00:54.400 --> 01:01.180
Our mission today is to decode a really brilliant, highly mathematical paper published in November 2026.

01:01.720 --> 01:03.660
Yeah, it's by the independent researcher Flyxion.

01:04.060 --> 01:04.320
Right.

01:04.560 --> 01:08.300
And it's titled Conditional Optimality Under Scalar Objectives.

01:08.940 --> 01:11.480
And just for you listening, I want to set the tone here.

01:11.600 --> 01:15.000
This is a pure, impartial look at the machinery of decision-making.

01:15.300 --> 01:16.960
Yeah, we aren't taking political sides here.

01:17.180 --> 01:20.580
And we're not here to debunk utilitarianism or anything like that.

01:20.740 --> 01:21.420
No, not at all.

01:21.420 --> 01:27.360
We are here to look at the engine itself because whether you are looking at the effective altruism movement,

01:27.580 --> 01:30.220
you know, trying to rank charities to do the most good per dollar,

01:30.360 --> 01:36.480
or you're looking at the frontier of AI governance, we are using the exact same kind of scalar math.

01:37.400 --> 01:42.440
And this paper proves mathematically that the numbers alone can never tell you what to do without,

01:42.740 --> 01:44.920
well, secretly relying on human assumptions.

01:44.920 --> 01:49.180
It's really a phenomenal piece of work because it just strips away all the ideology.

01:49.620 --> 01:49.780
Yeah.

01:49.940 --> 01:50.960
It just looks at the axioms.

01:51.420 --> 01:56.280
You know, when you ask an algorithm to maximize a single number, a scalar objective,

01:56.680 --> 01:59.460
what happens to all those variables you left out?

01:59.700 --> 01:59.960
Right.

02:00.080 --> 02:01.620
What happens to the noise in your data?

02:01.780 --> 02:02.180
Exactly.

02:02.180 --> 02:09.060
And the paper demonstrates that optimizing a flawed metric doesn't just give you like a slightly flawed result.

02:09.520 --> 02:15.000
It can actively engineer a complete catastrophe while literally telling you everything is perfect.

02:15.540 --> 02:15.660
Wow.

02:15.940 --> 02:17.120
Okay, let's unpack this.

02:17.340 --> 02:21.080
Let's start with the absolute baseline equation for doing good in the world.

02:21.260 --> 02:22.340
Expected value.

02:22.340 --> 02:22.700
Oh, yeah.

02:23.060 --> 02:23.580
The classic.

02:24.380 --> 02:28.400
If you spend any time in the philanthropic space, especially in those effective altruism debates,

02:28.620 --> 02:30.240
you see this formula everywhere.

02:30.680 --> 02:38.700
It's the value of an action equals the probability of an outcome multiplied by the utility or the moral value of that outcome.

02:38.900 --> 02:39.060
Right.

02:39.160 --> 02:40.760
So V equals P times U.

02:41.300 --> 02:43.980
It is basically the cornerstone of rational choice theory.

02:44.340 --> 02:46.340
And, I mean, it seems perfectly logical, right?

02:47.020 --> 02:51.260
But let's apply it to a real debate happening right now, which is long-termism.

02:51.260 --> 02:52.900
Oh, this is a perfect example.

02:53.420 --> 02:59.500
You have people arguing over whether we should spend, say, $100 million today on malaria nets,

02:59.760 --> 03:05.500
which we know with near 100% certainty will save a specific number of lives this year.

03:06.120 --> 03:15.300
Or should we spend that exact same money trying to prevent a hypothetical rogue AI from destroying humanity a thousand years from now?

03:15.580 --> 03:19.520
And this is exactly where Flyxion points out the first big mathematical vulnerability.

03:19.520 --> 03:22.440
It's called speculative benefit domination.

03:22.940 --> 03:22.960
Okay.

03:23.060 --> 03:27.860
Let's break that down for the listener because I really want to make sure the mechanics of this are crystal clear.

03:28.060 --> 03:28.140
Sure.

03:28.140 --> 03:37.160
So if I'm calculating the welfare of trillions of hypothetical future humans, the U in that equation, the utility, it just becomes unimaginably massive.

03:37.420 --> 03:37.660
Right.

03:37.760 --> 03:39.280
It basically approaches infinity.

03:39.520 --> 03:39.700
Yeah.

03:40.140 --> 03:45.500
And because the equation is just simple multiplication, a tiny, highly speculative probability.

03:45.500 --> 03:49.760
Like, say, a 0.0000001% chance?

03:49.880 --> 03:50.420
Yeah, exactly.

03:50.800 --> 03:52.920
A tiny chance of saving a trillion lives.

03:53.120 --> 03:59.880
You multiply that by that massive utility and it yields an expected benefit of tens of thousands of lives.

04:00.040 --> 04:00.500
Oh, wow.

04:00.500 --> 04:08.960
So mathematically, that highly speculative intervention instantly outweighs the absolute certainty of saving a few thousand people from malaria today.

04:09.880 --> 04:11.820
But wait, I can hear the pushback already.

04:11.980 --> 04:19.320
I mean, on behalf of you listening, someone is going to say, well, isn't a tiny chance to save a trillion people mathematically worth it?

04:19.560 --> 04:19.820
Right.

04:20.000 --> 04:21.560
The cosmic lottery ticket argument.

04:21.740 --> 04:22.040
Exactly.

04:22.240 --> 04:26.520
Like buying a cosmic lottery ticket where the payout is the survival of the human race.

04:26.700 --> 04:29.680
If the payoff is that massive, the math says you take the bet.

04:29.680 --> 04:36.660
What's fascinating here is that, yes, the math says you take the bet, but only if you assume the probability is an objective fact.

04:37.200 --> 04:37.480
Oh.

04:37.760 --> 04:44.260
And Flyxion forces us to separate the logical possibility of an event from the actual evidential support for its probability.

04:44.540 --> 04:44.940
I see.

04:45.420 --> 04:53.660
Just because a forecasting model spits out a probability of, you know, 10 to the negative sixth, that doesn't mean the real world operates on that fraction.

04:53.820 --> 04:54.140
Right.

04:54.140 --> 05:05.100
The math itself doesn't know the difference between a probability derived from centuries of hard empirical data and a probability somebody basically just made up because it felt right in a thought experiment.

05:05.880 --> 05:07.840
So the spreadsheet just sees the number.

05:08.020 --> 05:11.540
It doesn't see how fragile the evidence behind the number is.

05:11.620 --> 05:11.940
Exactly.

05:12.640 --> 05:15.440
The structural math has no built-in defense against that fragility.

05:15.440 --> 05:19.600
And this is where the paper brings in the Maximin criterion.

05:19.800 --> 05:19.820
Okay.

05:20.060 --> 05:20.460
Maximin.

05:20.700 --> 05:20.880
Yeah.

05:21.000 --> 05:25.340
Which has roots in Ellsberg's paradox and the economics of ambiguity aversion.

05:25.840 --> 05:31.000
Walk us through Ellsberg's paradox because I think that perfectly illustrates what we're actually dealing with here.

05:31.400 --> 05:31.640
Okay.

05:31.960 --> 05:35.480
Imagine an urn, like a jar, with 90 balls in it.

05:35.760 --> 05:36.260
90 balls.

05:36.360 --> 05:36.580
Got it.

05:36.580 --> 05:38.920
And I tell you that exactly 30 of them are red.

05:39.840 --> 05:44.940
The remaining 60 are a mix of black and yellow, but I absolutely refuse to tell you the ratio.

05:45.300 --> 05:45.440
Okay.

05:45.540 --> 05:49.280
So it could be 60 black and zero yellow or zero black and 60 yellow.

05:49.420 --> 05:49.580
Right.

05:49.780 --> 05:50.980
Or anything in between.

05:51.440 --> 05:57.020
So I have solid evidence for the red balls and complete ambiguity for the black and yellow ones.

05:57.140 --> 05:57.400
Yes.

05:57.860 --> 05:59.220
Now, I offer you a bet.

06:00.180 --> 06:03.020
Option A, you win $100 if you pull a red ball.

06:03.020 --> 06:06.820
Option B, you win $100 if you pull a black ball.

06:07.340 --> 06:08.880
I mean, I'd choose the red ball.

06:09.220 --> 06:10.440
Most people choose the red ball.

06:10.780 --> 06:11.080
Why?

06:11.840 --> 06:14.300
Because the 1 in 3 probability is known.

06:14.960 --> 06:17.780
They avoid the black ball because the probability is ambiguous.

06:18.580 --> 06:25.580
Even though logically the expected value of pulling a black ball could be higher if there happen to be like 60 of them in there.

06:25.980 --> 06:26.460
Exactly.

06:26.980 --> 06:27.860
We hate ambiguity.

06:28.480 --> 06:30.000
We really want to know the odds.

06:30.280 --> 06:30.520
Right.

06:30.520 --> 06:38.660
But expected value math, that V equals P times U equation, it structurally assumes you are totally neutral to ambiguity.

06:39.120 --> 06:39.820
Oh, I see.

06:40.040 --> 06:45.760
It assumes you treat a known probability and a totally speculative guess exactly the same way.

06:45.960 --> 06:47.840
Which is wild when you think about it.

06:47.900 --> 06:48.280
It is.

06:48.280 --> 06:56.000
So, what a Maximin criterion does is it looks at the entire range of possible probabilities based on your actual evidence.

06:56.420 --> 06:56.660
Okay.

06:56.880 --> 07:05.180
If your evidence for, say, an AI doom scenario is so incredibly weak and speculative that the lower bound of your confidence interval is just zero.

07:05.280 --> 07:08.260
Then under Maximin, you evaluate that worst case scenario.

07:08.480 --> 07:08.720
Yes.

07:09.320 --> 07:12.060
And zero times a trillion is zero.

07:12.420 --> 07:14.060
The expected value just vanishes.

07:14.420 --> 07:15.420
It completely vanishes.

07:15.420 --> 07:27.080
And if that speculative bet requires diverting funds from guaranteed interventions, meaning there's a certain harm or, you know, a real opportunity cost today, the expected value actually goes negative.

07:27.320 --> 07:28.000
Wow.

07:28.320 --> 07:35.980
Flyxion's point is that choosing between standard additive expected value and a Maximin rule is not just a mathematical preliminary.

07:35.980 --> 07:39.340
It is a profound ethical and epistemic choice.

07:39.340 --> 07:44.680
So, you literally have to decide how you value evidence before you let the math multiply the numbers.

07:44.820 --> 07:45.260
Exactly.

07:46.140 --> 07:47.880
The math will not make that judgment for you.

07:48.060 --> 07:52.160
So, you have to basically smuggle your epistemology into the spreadsheet.

07:52.640 --> 07:52.940
Mm-hmm.

07:53.180 --> 07:56.420
Which actually leads us directly into the next massive hurdle.

07:57.020 --> 08:00.160
Let's say we somehow get past the probability problem.

08:00.700 --> 08:03.600
We still have to figure out how to calculate the U, the utility.

08:03.900 --> 08:04.140
Right.

08:04.300 --> 08:05.180
The benefit itself.

08:05.180 --> 08:05.540
Yeah.

08:05.880 --> 08:13.500
And to get to a massive number of lives or to measure the impact of a huge economic policy, you have to add different people's welfare together.

08:13.760 --> 08:13.960
Right.

08:14.220 --> 08:15.380
Interpersonal aggregation.

08:15.900 --> 08:20.240
This is honestly one of the thorniest problems in all of economics and moral philosophy.

08:21.000 --> 08:27.940
The paper calls this the representation dependence of the unweighted sum, which, I mean, sounds incredibly dense.

08:28.100 --> 08:29.560
It does sound a bit intimidating.

08:29.560 --> 08:34.360
But the underlying concept is something we all intuitively experience every day, right?

08:34.760 --> 08:40.300
It's the realization that my internal experience of happiness or suffering is locked inside my head.

08:40.640 --> 08:41.860
And mine is locked inside mine.

08:42.080 --> 08:42.420
Exactly.

08:42.660 --> 08:44.380
So, how do we add them?

08:44.900 --> 08:52.640
Well, in classical expected utility theory, an individual's utility scale is only defined up to what we call a positive affine transformation.

08:53.040 --> 08:53.220
Okay.

08:53.320 --> 08:55.220
Let's unpack positive affine transformation.

08:55.760 --> 08:58.840
Let's say we're trying to measure how much we both value a cup of coffee.

08:58.840 --> 08:59.240
Sure.

08:59.500 --> 09:07.300
Inside your own personal decision-making process, your scale is completely arbitrary, as long as the order of your preferences remains consistent.

09:07.500 --> 09:07.820
Okay.

09:08.400 --> 09:11.320
Let's say you prefer coffee over tea and tea over water.

09:11.820 --> 09:15.980
You could assign coffee a value of 3, tea a 2, and water a 1.

09:16.180 --> 09:17.880
That's just a simple 1-2-3 scale.

09:17.920 --> 09:18.340
Makes sense.

09:18.680 --> 09:24.640
But you could also take that exact same internal preference and multiply it by 1,000, and then, I don't know, add 500.

09:24.640 --> 09:25.120
Okay.

09:25.240 --> 09:29.540
So, coffee is 3,500, tea is 2,500, water is 1,500.

09:29.800 --> 09:30.060
Yes.

09:30.680 --> 09:33.420
That is a positive affine transformation.

09:33.980 --> 09:38.700
And mathematically, it changes absolutely nothing about the choices you will make in the real world.

09:39.040 --> 09:41.180
Because I will still always pick the coffee.

09:41.660 --> 09:42.080
Exactly.

09:42.600 --> 09:47.180
For modeling an individual's rational choices, the scale itself is totally meaningless.

09:47.620 --> 09:50.080
Only the relative distances between the options matter.

09:50.080 --> 09:50.640
Okay.

09:50.820 --> 09:52.700
So, I have my own internal thermostat.

09:53.300 --> 09:53.840
Yes, you do.

09:54.460 --> 09:56.700
But here is the trapdoor Flyxion highlights.

09:57.180 --> 10:05.260
What happens when a central algorithm tries to add your utility and my utility together to maximize social welfare?

10:05.620 --> 10:10.260
It blindly takes whatever arbitrary scale we happen to be using and just jams them together.

10:10.480 --> 10:10.900
Precisely.

10:11.260 --> 10:13.280
Let's say we were deciding on the office temperature.

10:13.900 --> 10:17.560
I might be using a very modest 1 to 10 scale for my comfort.

10:17.980 --> 10:18.280
Right.

10:18.280 --> 10:22.200
But you, internally, might be using scale from 1 to 1,000.

10:22.820 --> 10:24.220
Okay, I see where this is going.

10:24.820 --> 10:29.020
If the building's optimization algorithm just takes an unweighted sum of our raw numbers together,

10:29.440 --> 10:32.780
it will always prioritize your preferences and freeze me out.

10:33.140 --> 10:36.240
Just simply because my internal math uses bigger numbers.

10:36.360 --> 10:36.560
Yes.

10:37.220 --> 10:40.360
You could multiply one person's utility scale by a constant,

10:40.740 --> 10:44.560
which, remember, is mathematically perfectly valid for their individual choices.

10:44.660 --> 10:47.960
But doing so completely reverses the outcome of the social total.

10:47.960 --> 10:48.400
Exactly.

10:49.400 --> 10:53.940
The math is entirely silent on how to compare one unit of your welfare against one unit of mine.

10:54.740 --> 10:58.320
Economists like Harsanyi and Sen have debated this for decades.

10:59.120 --> 11:02.740
And Flyxion brings it right to the forefront of these optimization algorithms.

11:02.980 --> 11:03.180
Right.

11:03.380 --> 11:07.640
If you want to add welfare together, you have to introduce a conversion rate.

11:07.640 --> 11:14.480
You have to make a deeply ethical judgment about how my subjective experience literally compares to yours.

11:14.980 --> 11:19.000
And, man, it gets infinitely harder when you try to scale this up to global policy.

11:19.280 --> 11:24.980
I mean, how do you compare the utility of a billionaire getting a tax cut to a family getting food assistance?

11:24.980 --> 11:25.740
You can't.

11:26.140 --> 11:27.080
Not with pure math.

11:27.540 --> 11:31.560
Or how do you compare human welfare to the welfare of, like, factory farmed animals?

11:31.840 --> 11:37.320
Or the welfare of people alive today versus unborn generations in the year 3000?

11:37.720 --> 11:39.800
You just cannot do it with pure math.

11:40.200 --> 11:43.960
You have to establish an ethical premise of comparability first.

11:44.200 --> 11:44.480
Right.

11:44.480 --> 11:49.900
The algorithm cannot output a moral truth if you didn't input a moral conversion rate.

11:50.780 --> 11:50.800
Okay.

11:50.980 --> 11:56.640
So if the math can't ethically compare my happiness to yours without us intervening,

11:56.840 --> 12:02.560
how do philanthropic algorithms or government agencies even build their spreadsheets in the first place?

12:03.160 --> 12:04.380
Well, they have to create a menu.

12:04.580 --> 12:04.800
Right.

12:05.100 --> 12:08.160
Because they have to start by picking a finite list of options to look at.

12:08.280 --> 12:11.180
They can't evaluate every possible action in the universe.

12:11.180 --> 12:13.820
And this brings us to the concept of the candidate set.

12:14.480 --> 12:20.580
Which, honestly, might contain the most counterintuitive, mind-bending mathematical proof in the entire paper.

12:20.700 --> 12:20.940
Okay.

12:21.120 --> 12:22.140
Let's dive into it.

12:22.340 --> 12:28.640
Because usually we assume that if an evaluator looks at a list of charities and picks the one with the highest mathematical score,

12:28.760 --> 12:30.320
they've found the best option.

12:30.720 --> 12:30.980
Right.

12:31.480 --> 12:34.440
They have found the best option on that specific list.

12:34.780 --> 12:35.040
Mm-hmm.

12:35.040 --> 12:39.000
The paper uses two distinct symbols here that are crucial to keep straight.

12:40.020 --> 12:44.080
Omega represents the universe of all possible options.

12:44.080 --> 12:48.160
So every charity, every policy, every conceivable action humanity could take.

12:48.260 --> 12:48.720
Exactly.

12:48.940 --> 12:49.140
Okay.

12:49.220 --> 12:49.700
That's omega.

12:50.000 --> 12:53.880
Then you have the letter S, which represents the candidate set.

12:54.400 --> 12:58.320
The tiny subset of options we actually put on the spreadsheet to evaluate.

12:58.700 --> 12:58.960
Right.

12:59.500 --> 13:00.320
Omega is everything.

13:00.640 --> 13:01.640
S is our menu.

13:02.640 --> 13:07.380
Now, when an algorithm optimizes, it only searches over S.

13:07.900 --> 13:08.720
Makes sense.

13:08.720 --> 13:15.880
But finding the absolute mathematical maximum within set S guarantees absolutely nothing about your success over omega.

13:16.280 --> 13:16.580
Right.

13:17.080 --> 13:23.520
The only way you can mathematically certify that the winner of your small menu is actually the best overall choice

13:23.520 --> 13:29.880
is if you can put a hard mathematical limit on the value of every single alternative you didn't look at.

13:29.880 --> 13:30.800
Oh, wow.

13:31.040 --> 13:33.580
Flyxion calls this the certification condition, right?

13:33.800 --> 13:34.040
Yes.

13:34.140 --> 13:38.920
Meaning I can say this is the most effective climate intervention on my list,

13:39.040 --> 13:43.820
but I can never scientifically claim this is the most effective climate intervention, period.

13:44.200 --> 13:49.800
Unless you have mathematical proof that every idea you didn't test is objectively worse,

13:50.200 --> 13:52.560
which is frankly impossible in the real world.

13:52.560 --> 13:54.040
It is practically impossible.

13:54.880 --> 14:00.320
And this introduces what the paper refers to using concepts from information theory as perplexity.

14:00.560 --> 14:00.660
Yeah.

14:01.080 --> 14:03.820
And perplexity isn't just a feeling of being confused.

14:04.060 --> 14:06.800
It's a measurable statistical state.

14:07.160 --> 14:08.900
Explain perplexity for us then.

14:09.380 --> 14:12.180
In this context, perplexity is related to entropy.

14:12.620 --> 14:17.860
It's a way of measuring the effective number of equally likely alternatives in a probability distribution.

14:18.160 --> 14:18.420
Okay.

14:18.420 --> 14:23.180
When you are trying to find the best intervention, you really have two types of uncertainty.

14:23.740 --> 14:25.180
You have ranking uncertainty.

14:25.580 --> 14:26.420
Which is the question...

14:27.060 --> 14:28.960
Did I measure the things on my menu correctly?

14:29.200 --> 14:31.640
Like, is option A actually better than option B?

14:32.080 --> 14:32.520
Exactly.

14:32.840 --> 14:34.340
That's just standard margin of error.

14:34.720 --> 14:36.740
But then you have omission uncertainty.

14:37.400 --> 14:37.940
Which is what?

14:38.220 --> 14:43.620
It's the sheer probability mass that the true actual best option is sitting off the menu entirely.

14:43.920 --> 14:44.260
Uh-huh.

14:44.260 --> 14:51.280
Perplexity forces you to acknowledge the dispersion of probability across that vast, unmeasured space of omega.

14:51.800 --> 14:52.020
Okay.

14:52.080 --> 15:01.480
But if omission uncertainty is the problem, if the danger is that the best idea isn't on our menu, my immediate intuition is that we just need a bigger menu.

15:01.600 --> 15:01.760
Right.

15:02.240 --> 15:03.200
That's what everyone thinks.

15:03.440 --> 15:03.660
Yeah.

15:03.660 --> 15:12.340
If I'm a massive philanthropic foundation, I should just hire a hundred more researchers and evaluate 10,000 charities instead of 50.

15:12.960 --> 15:14.800
Expand the set S.

15:15.500 --> 15:16.820
That is the universal intuition.

15:17.300 --> 15:20.560
If you want to find the best thing, search a wider area.

15:21.080 --> 15:21.940
Evaluate more candidates.

15:22.200 --> 15:22.300
Sure.

15:22.580 --> 15:26.200
But this is where Flyxion drops the hammer with the optimizer's curse.

15:27.000 --> 15:27.460
Oh, man.

15:28.240 --> 15:29.860
This blew my mind when I read it.

15:29.980 --> 15:30.440
It's incredible.

15:30.440 --> 15:34.740
Well, searching more actually introduces massive statistical selection bias.

15:35.140 --> 15:38.060
The idea that having more options can be mathematically worse.

15:38.700 --> 15:43.100
Walk me through a concrete scenario here, step by step, because it feels like a total paradox.

15:43.720 --> 15:45.840
How can trying harder make you dumber?

15:46.200 --> 15:46.420
Okay.

15:46.600 --> 15:48.540
Let's build like a mental spreadsheet.

15:49.220 --> 15:52.620
Imagine you are evaluating a thousand different charitable interventions.

15:52.960 --> 15:53.260
Okay.

15:53.300 --> 15:54.100
A thousand charities.

15:54.100 --> 16:03.140
And let's assume, just for the sake of the thought experiment, that in reality, every single one of these interventions produces the exact same amount of true good.

16:03.400 --> 16:03.680
Okay.

16:03.880 --> 16:06.820
They all have a true value of exactly 100 points.

16:07.060 --> 16:08.880
So the reality is a flat tie.

16:08.980 --> 16:10.440
They are all equally good.

16:10.800 --> 16:11.260
Right.

16:11.900 --> 16:13.780
But you don't know the true value.

16:14.320 --> 16:16.460
You only have your measurements, your estimates.

16:16.920 --> 16:18.880
And no measurement in the real world is perfect.

16:19.340 --> 16:19.720
Exactly.

16:20.160 --> 16:22.100
Your researchers have noise in their data.

16:22.100 --> 16:26.780
They might overestimate by 20 points or underestimate by 30 points.

16:27.220 --> 16:29.760
Let's say the noise is randomly distributed.

16:30.520 --> 16:33.760
So for charity A, the true value is 100, but we measure it as 90.

16:34.160 --> 16:36.400
Charity B is 100, but we measure it as 115.

16:37.080 --> 16:37.440
Yes.

16:37.880 --> 16:40.340
Now, what does the optimization algorithm do?

16:40.720 --> 16:45.440
It looks at your list of 1,000 estimated values, and it simply picks the highest number.

16:45.840 --> 16:46.060
Wait.

16:46.140 --> 16:51.320
So it's going to pick the charity where the researchers made the largest positive mistake?

16:51.320 --> 16:52.200
Precisely.

16:52.420 --> 16:58.660
If you search through 1,000 options, the maximum estimated value you find isn't going to be 100.

16:58.820 --> 17:01.720
It's going to be 140 or 150.

17:02.040 --> 17:06.380
Driven entirely by the extreme right tail of the statistical noise distribution.

17:06.560 --> 17:06.820
Yes.

17:07.340 --> 17:11.120
You're mathematically selecting for the highest positive measurement error.

17:11.120 --> 17:12.740
You aren't finding the best charity.

17:12.980 --> 17:15.780
You are finding the most overly optimistic spreadsheet.

17:15.960 --> 17:16.240
Exactly.

17:16.940 --> 17:19.780
Now, watch what happens when you expand the candidate set.

17:20.220 --> 17:25.260
Let's say you increase your search from 1,000 charities to 100,000 charities.

17:25.380 --> 17:25.560
Okay.

17:25.780 --> 17:28.200
The true value of all of them is still 100.

17:29.060 --> 17:37.220
But because you were taking a massive number of random samples of noise, the chances of generating an even more extreme positive error skyrocket.

17:37.220 --> 17:38.160
Oh, jeez.

17:38.320 --> 17:41.760
The algorithm might now pick a winner with an estimated value of 180.

17:42.080 --> 17:48.880
So the gap between what we think we are achieving and what we are actually achieving gets wider the more options we evaluate.

17:49.100 --> 17:49.340
Yes.

17:49.880 --> 17:51.840
This is the optimizer's curse.

17:51.840 --> 18:06.340
The mathematical proof shows a devastating scenario where adding a genuinely better option into your candidate set, alongside thousands of useless options with noisy estimates, it can actually lower the expected true value of what you ultimately select.

18:06.760 --> 18:07.340
Wait, really?

18:07.720 --> 18:11.600
Even if the best option is actually on the menu, the noise can bury it.

18:11.740 --> 18:11.980
Yes.

18:12.700 --> 18:18.980
Let's say you have one amazing charity with a true value of 130, and you measure it pretty accurately at 135.

18:19.360 --> 18:20.380
Okay, that's a great charity.

18:20.380 --> 18:27.120
But you also evaluate 10,000 mediocre charities with a true value of 80, but they have massive measurement noise.

18:27.440 --> 18:27.700
Right.

18:28.280 --> 18:37.740
Because you are pulling 10,000 samples of noise, statistical probability dictates that at least one of those mediocre charities is going to have a wildly inaccurate measurement of, say, 140.

18:38.140 --> 18:40.520
And the algorithm blindly picks the 140.

18:40.920 --> 18:41.200
Yes.

18:41.800 --> 18:49.620
So by expanding the menu to include all those mediocre, noisy options, you mathematically guarantee that you will overlook the genuinely good charity.

18:49.620 --> 18:51.500
The noise overwhelms the signal.

18:52.140 --> 18:58.120
As the candidate set grows, the maximum estimated value diverges aggressively from the maximum true value.

18:58.340 --> 18:58.640
Wow.

18:59.120 --> 19:03.440
The optimization process itself generates a systematic upward bias.

19:03.880 --> 19:06.840
So expanding the menu doesn't just cost time and money.

19:07.080 --> 19:10.260
It mathematically degrades the reliability of your decision.

19:10.580 --> 19:10.980
Correct.

19:10.980 --> 19:16.500
Unless your measurements are flawless, which they never are, the math punishes you for looking too widely.

19:17.200 --> 19:21.220
It really forces you to realize that optimization isn't just about finding the peak of a mountain.

19:21.620 --> 19:23.620
It's about navigating a fog of variance.

19:24.100 --> 19:27.440
And if you just follow the highest perceived elevation in the fog...

19:27.440 --> 19:31.360
You are probably just walking up a statistical anomaly, not the actual summit.

19:31.360 --> 19:33.520
That is just profound.

19:33.880 --> 19:34.240
Mm-hmm.

19:34.660 --> 19:40.940
And, I mean, it gets even more complicated when we realize that the items on these menus aren't just isolated islands, right?

19:41.300 --> 19:41.560
Right.

19:41.620 --> 19:42.320
They aren't independent.

19:42.820 --> 19:46.360
In the real world, interventions interact with each other.

19:46.540 --> 19:48.320
They rely on each other.

19:48.760 --> 19:52.020
Which is the focus of the next major mathematical breakdown in the paper.

19:52.520 --> 19:55.060
The failure of additive separability.

19:55.640 --> 19:58.640
Or what we might call the myth of the lone hero.

19:58.640 --> 20:03.600
I found the example Flyxion used here to be incredibly illuminating.

20:03.800 --> 20:11.440
It dismantles the whole assumption that an intervention has a standalone marginal value that you can just add up, like building blocks.

20:12.100 --> 20:14.760
Yeah, let's talk about the scientist, the teacher, and the technician.

20:15.540 --> 20:15.820
Yes.

20:15.980 --> 20:18.160
Let's walk through the math of this complementarity.

20:19.100 --> 20:21.320
Imagine you are a funding agency.

20:21.640 --> 20:25.860
And you have a budget to fund exactly two out of three possible interventions.

20:25.860 --> 20:26.160
Okay.

20:26.160 --> 20:29.380
Intervention one is paying for a brilliant teacher.

20:30.200 --> 20:33.500
Intervention two is paying for a skilled laboratory technician.

20:34.380 --> 20:35.060
And intervention three?

20:35.500 --> 20:40.140
Intervention three is paying for a totally unrelated feel-good local project.

20:40.260 --> 20:41.920
Let's say planting a community garden.

20:42.740 --> 20:47.120
Now, a standard optimization algorithm evaluates these one at a time to assign a score.

20:47.500 --> 20:52.720
If you just fund the teacher, but there is no lab and no genius student, the scientific output is zero.

20:52.720 --> 20:56.420
Right. The standalone value of intervention one is zero.

20:56.620 --> 21:01.840
If you just fund the technician, but there is no teacher and no student, the output is also zero.

21:02.060 --> 21:05.040
The standalone value of intervention two is zero.

21:05.040 --> 21:11.460
But if you fund the community garden, intervention three, it produces a small, independent amount of good all on its own.

21:11.600 --> 21:13.680
Let's call it five units of value.

21:14.320 --> 21:15.880
Now, what does the algorithm do?

21:16.700 --> 21:18.540
It ranks them by their standalone scores.

21:18.940 --> 21:19.180
Right.

21:19.560 --> 21:21.840
Intervention three is in first place with five points.

21:22.460 --> 21:25.180
Interventions one and two are tied for last place with zero points.

21:25.180 --> 21:34.880
So, the algorithm selects intervention three, plants the garden, and then, I guess, randomly picks either the teacher or the tech with the remaining budget, and it secures five units of value.

21:35.240 --> 21:35.580
Exactly.

21:35.940 --> 21:38.400
But it completely missed the massive counterfactual space.

21:38.780 --> 21:43.000
Because if it had selected intervention one and intervention two together...

21:43.000 --> 21:43.860
The teacher and the tech.

21:44.080 --> 21:44.260
Yeah.

21:44.360 --> 21:49.620
They would have trained a genius scientist who cures a disease, yielding like 10,000 units of value.

21:49.780 --> 21:51.760
Because their value is entirely complementary.

21:52.360 --> 21:53.760
They only work when paired.

21:54.020 --> 21:54.280
Right.

21:54.280 --> 22:00.860
But an algorithm that evaluates isolated, marginal impacts will systematically fail to discover synergistic value.

22:01.540 --> 22:03.360
And this isn't just some theoretical puzzle.

22:03.480 --> 22:05.520
This happens in real-world policy all the time.

22:05.700 --> 22:06.160
All the time.

22:06.740 --> 22:11.740
We evaluate educational tools, economic subsidies, and healthcare programs in total isolation.

22:12.220 --> 22:12.460
Yep.

22:12.880 --> 22:17.900
When interventions have strong complementarity, ranking them individually fails disastrously.

22:17.900 --> 22:24.740
Summing up individually estimated values is just a mathematically doomed strategy for complex social systems.

22:24.740 --> 22:29.940
It assumes the world is a simple addition problem when it's actually a web of chemical reactions.

22:29.940 --> 22:32.900
And that ties directly into the problem of causal inference.

22:33.080 --> 22:38.200
Because when we try to figure out what works, we usually just look at observational data, right?

22:38.200 --> 22:41.340
We look at what is already happening and try to copy it.

22:41.340 --> 22:46.120
And Flyxion demonstrates exactly why that is deeply dangerous.

22:46.120 --> 22:56.620
Identifying true counterfactual values, meaning what would have happened if we didn't intervene, is notoriously difficult because of selection bias in observational data.

22:56.620 --> 23:00.300
Let's use the advanced math program example from the text.

23:00.500 --> 23:00.740
Okay.

23:01.200 --> 23:10.520
Say a school district looks at its data and observes that kids who are placed into an advanced calculus track go on to have incredibly successful high-earning careers.

23:11.060 --> 23:13.920
The observable data shows a massive correlation.

23:14.180 --> 23:14.340
Right.

23:15.100 --> 23:22.980
A naive optimization model will look at that data and conclude that the causal value of the calculus program is astronomical.

23:22.980 --> 23:31.460
So, it will recommend defunding other programs to force every single student into advanced calculus, assuming it will yield the same success rate across the board.

23:31.800 --> 23:34.500
But that completely ignores selection bias.

23:34.900 --> 23:35.460
Entirely.

23:36.300 --> 23:43.040
Flyxion shows mathematically that two completely opposed causal models can produce the exact same observational dataset.

23:43.280 --> 23:43.600
Really?

23:43.980 --> 23:44.180
Yeah.

23:44.640 --> 23:44.880
How?

23:45.260 --> 23:49.580
Well, Model A asserts that the calculus program actively causes this success.

23:49.820 --> 23:51.960
It imparts skills that make kids successful.

23:51.960 --> 23:54.100
That's the intuitive one.

23:54.480 --> 23:55.200
And Model B?

23:55.560 --> 24:04.120
Model B asserts that the success is predetermined by natural aptitude or parental support or socioeconomic status.

24:04.520 --> 24:10.660
And the calculus program merely acts as a filter that selects kids who were already destined to succeed anyway.

24:10.860 --> 24:11.380
Oh, wow.

24:11.500 --> 24:11.660
Yeah.

24:11.740 --> 24:13.840
And both models fit the data perfectly.

24:13.840 --> 24:14.640
Perfectly.

24:14.800 --> 24:23.560
If you just look at the spreadsheet, you cannot distinguish between an intervention that generates value and an intervention that simply selects for pre-existing value.

24:23.560 --> 24:26.660
Think about the implications of that for philanthropy in business.

24:27.120 --> 24:32.460
We look at a successful microloan program in a developing country and we say, the data proves this works.

24:32.960 --> 24:42.000
But what if the data just proves that the people who apply for and secure microloans are inherently the most entrepreneurial driven individuals in that village?

24:42.000 --> 24:44.600
And they would have found a way to succeed regardless.

24:45.100 --> 24:45.480
Exactly.

24:45.940 --> 24:48.040
This raises an important question for you, the listener.

24:48.580 --> 24:53.080
How much of the success data we rely on is just observational masking?

24:53.460 --> 24:53.740
Right.

24:53.740 --> 25:01.900
If we blindly optimize based on observational correlation, we end up funding massive interventions that actually produce zero marginal utility.

25:02.380 --> 25:04.680
Because they are just taking credit for natural momentum.

25:04.900 --> 25:05.320
Exactly.

25:06.080 --> 25:17.440
So if we can't trust the optimizer's curse on big menus, and we can't trust individual evaluations because of complementarity, and we can't trust observational data because of selection bias, what do we do?

25:17.820 --> 25:20.740
Often institutions just fall back on asking people.

25:20.740 --> 25:26.920
Right. They issue grants, they take proposals, and they ask project leaders, how much good will you do and how much will it cost?

25:27.420 --> 25:32.980
And that introduces human behavior into the mathematics, which is where things get truly destructive.

25:33.700 --> 25:36.700
This brings us to optimization over claims.

25:37.460 --> 25:44.880
Okay. This revolves around cost, feasibility, and a really crucial mathematical error called the continuity assumption, right?

25:45.080 --> 25:49.840
Yes. Referencing the work of Coté and Stewart on threshold scales.

25:49.840 --> 25:55.240
Let's explain the recycling factory example, because this trapdoor is so practical and so common.

25:55.720 --> 26:01.060
Yeah. When algorithms optimize budgets, they love to use averages and marginal returns.

26:01.340 --> 26:03.860
They assume that reality is a continuous line.

26:04.120 --> 26:10.340
They assume that if it costs $1 million to build a recycling factory that processes 1 million tons of waste,

26:10.580 --> 26:15.360
then giving the project half a million dollars will allow them to process half a million tons of waste.

26:15.800 --> 26:17.720
It assumes you can just scale everything linearly.

26:17.720 --> 26:22.220
Right. But in reality, half a budget doesn't get you half a functioning factory.

26:22.740 --> 26:26.500
It gets you a concrete slab, a half-finished roof, and a bankrupt contractor.

26:26.940 --> 26:28.620
It processes zero waste.

26:28.680 --> 26:29.160
Exactly.

26:29.860 --> 26:31.460
That is the threshold scale.

26:32.180 --> 26:36.620
The intervention requires a minimum critical mass of funding to yield any value whatsoever.

26:37.080 --> 26:37.440
Okay.

26:37.440 --> 26:42.700
If an optimizer is using marginal figures to divide up a budget across multiple projects,

26:42.700 --> 26:47.840
it will systematically defund threshold projects right below their critical breaking point,

26:48.460 --> 26:52.180
turning what could have been massive value into complete deadweight loss.

26:52.180 --> 26:59.100
But the even deeper problem with optimizing over claims is what happens when people realize they are competing for that budget.

26:59.100 --> 27:00.840
This is the contractor analogy.

27:01.200 --> 27:04.880
Oh, this is one of the most mathematically rigorous proofs in the paper.

27:05.640 --> 27:12.500
It utilizes the properties of log concave density functions to demonstrate selection on favorable reporting error.

27:12.700 --> 27:15.220
Okay, let's break down log concave densities.

27:15.680 --> 27:20.840
Because earlier we talked about how selecting the highest estimated value selects for noise.

27:20.840 --> 27:24.980
This is the exact same dynamic applied to cost, right?

27:25.400 --> 27:28.020
Yes, but with human incentives added in.

27:28.140 --> 27:28.420
Mm-hmm.

27:28.520 --> 27:29.840
Here's where it gets really interesting.

27:30.140 --> 27:30.280
Yeah.

27:31.000 --> 27:32.300
It's like hiring a contractor.

27:32.640 --> 27:38.240
Imagine you are putting a major public works project out for bid or a philanthropic grant.

27:38.680 --> 27:41.400
You have 20 different organizations submitting proposals.

27:41.760 --> 27:41.960
Okay.

27:42.400 --> 27:48.080
And let's say the true objective cost to do this project right is around $20,000.

27:48.080 --> 27:51.960
In a perfect world, everyone bids $20,000.

27:52.260 --> 27:56.100
But in reality, there are two distinct probability distributions at play.

27:56.320 --> 27:56.520
Right.

27:57.020 --> 28:00.180
First, there is the distribution of their actual underlying efficiency.

28:00.920 --> 28:02.940
Maybe one contractor is slightly faster.

28:03.240 --> 28:05.120
So their true cost is $19,000.

28:05.700 --> 28:08.940
And second, there is the distribution of their reporting error.

28:09.120 --> 28:09.600
Exactly.

28:10.360 --> 28:12.020
Reporting error can be accidental.

28:12.400 --> 28:14.740
Maybe they forgot to price in the cost of copper wire.

28:15.100 --> 28:16.280
Or it can be intentional.

28:16.280 --> 28:21.320
They are wildly optimistic, or they are just flat out lying to win the bid.

28:21.900 --> 28:23.900
So a bid of $5,000 comes in.

28:24.580 --> 28:27.380
Now think about the math of those two distributions overlapping.

28:28.240 --> 28:36.960
A bid that is aggressively lower than the true mean is extremely unlikely to be driven purely by actual underlying efficiency.

28:37.460 --> 28:40.700
There is a physical limit to how cheap materials and labor can be.

28:41.080 --> 28:44.540
You can't just wish concrete to be 75% cheaper.

28:44.540 --> 28:48.600
But there is practically no limit to human reporting error.

28:49.100 --> 28:52.660
People can lie or miscalculate by massive margins.

28:52.880 --> 28:53.000
Right.

28:53.380 --> 29:02.740
So mathematically, the extreme left tail of the bidding distribution, the absolute lowest bids, are disproportionately composed of extreme reporting errors.

29:02.740 --> 29:08.340
The person who bids $5,000 for a $20,000 kitchen remodel isn't a wizard.

29:08.980 --> 29:12.040
The math actually proves we select for the highest error.

29:12.040 --> 29:12.640
Yes.

29:12.640 --> 29:22.840
The gap between the reported cost and the actual realizable cost, what the paper calls the shortfall, is maximized when you select the minimum reported cost.

29:23.240 --> 29:23.580
Wow.

29:24.000 --> 29:33.280
By designing your objective function to optimize for the lowest claim, you structurally filter out the honest, competent organizations who know what things actually cost.

29:33.280 --> 29:38.920
You are systematically filtering in the liars, the incompetents, and the extreme optimists.

29:39.040 --> 29:42.640
Your algorithm literally becomes a magnet for misrepresentation.

29:42.740 --> 29:44.740
It creates a race to the bottom of epistemics.

29:45.300 --> 29:46.820
And what is the only way to stop that?

29:46.960 --> 29:47.720
You have to check their work.

29:48.000 --> 29:48.900
You have to audit the claims.

29:49.500 --> 29:50.300
Independent verification.

29:50.680 --> 29:50.920
Mm-hmm.

29:51.280 --> 29:55.080
You have to go to their previous job sites and see if they actually built what they said they built.

29:55.080 --> 29:57.480
But verification costs resources.

29:58.000 --> 30:00.080
It costs time, money, and personnel.

30:00.520 --> 30:00.620
Right.

30:01.180 --> 30:13.480
And if you are an institution that is exclusively obsessed with maximizing your measured impact, getting the absolute highest score on your internal spreadsheet, then spending money on auditors starts to look like a mathematical waste.

30:13.600 --> 30:15.900
Which brings us to the institutional blind spot.

30:15.900 --> 30:26.000
This is where Flyxion shifts from looking at the math in a vacuum to looking at what happens to an organization that internalizes this math as its sole operating principle.

30:26.620 --> 30:33.640
And to understand this, we have to make a very sharp distinction between finding the mathematical optimum and having permission to execute it.

30:33.900 --> 30:36.900
The paper brings up the FTX cryptocurrency exchange here.

30:37.220 --> 30:38.800
I want to be clear for everyone listening.

30:38.980 --> 30:41.680
We are adhering strictly to the paper's analytical framing.

30:41.680 --> 30:46.060
We aren't here to psychoanalyze the founder who was convicted of fraud in 2024.

30:47.000 --> 30:51.720
Flyxion uses FTX as a pure structural example of an objective function running wild.

30:52.620 --> 31:01.380
The critical point is that maximizing an objective function, V of A, does not inherently grant what the paper defines as permitted of A.

31:02.100 --> 31:09.420
Meaning just because an action creates the highest expected value on your spreadsheet doesn't mean you are ethically or legally allowed to take it.

31:09.620 --> 31:09.800
Right.

31:09.800 --> 31:13.800
An objective function is just a mathematical topography.

31:14.220 --> 31:15.240
It seeks the highest peak.

31:15.700 --> 31:15.760
Yeah.

31:16.180 --> 31:22.580
It doesn't inherently contain concepts like fiduciary duty, consent, property rights, or the rule of law.

31:22.760 --> 31:25.580
Those are external categorical constraints.

31:26.260 --> 31:36.800
So if an agent is managing billions of dollars of customer deposits and they calculate that gambling those deposits on a high-risk venture will generate massive expected value for global charities...

31:36.800 --> 31:39.620
The math of expected value will cheer them on.

31:40.220 --> 31:42.920
The math will say, yes, that is the optimal path.

31:42.920 --> 31:56.540
If you do not hard-code permission constraints into the system, if you leave it up to the discretion of an agent whose only goal is to maximize the scalar metric, the math will happily recommend impermissible, catastrophic actions.

31:56.760 --> 31:57.040
Wow.

31:57.040 --> 32:02.220
The optimization process actively dissolves boundaries that aren't mathematically priced into its objective.

32:02.220 --> 32:07.120
The math doesn't have a conscience, so you can't assume the output will be conscientious.

32:07.740 --> 32:11.660
But man, the internal decay of these institutions is even more fascinating.

32:12.380 --> 32:16.400
The paper describes a phenomenon called endogenous verification decay.

32:16.400 --> 32:20.340
This is a brilliant modeling of institutional psychology using math.

32:20.480 --> 32:21.280
Tell us about it.

32:21.780 --> 32:31.600
Flyxion models an institution that has capital and it has verification capacity, meaning its ability to audit itself, catch its own errors, and monitor real-world harms.

32:31.740 --> 32:32.060
Okay.

32:32.780 --> 32:38.040
The institution's goal is to maximize its measure of reward, but here's the fatal flaw in the system.

32:38.940 --> 32:43.340
Verification catches errors, but it does not produce positive measured reward.

32:43.340 --> 32:46.420
Right. Auditing a charity doesn't save lives on paper.

32:46.540 --> 32:49.920
It just ensures the lives you thought you were saving are actually being saved.

32:50.520 --> 33:00.120
So if I'm a manager trying to hit my quarterly impact metrics and I look at my budget, I have $5 million in the auditing department and $5 million in the frontline intervention department.

33:00.600 --> 33:05.820
If I defund the auditors and move that money to the frontline, my measured output instantly doubles.

33:06.080 --> 33:08.640
And you look like a hero to the optimization algorithm.

33:08.840 --> 33:09.240
Unbelievable.

33:09.240 --> 33:20.940
Proposition 12 proves that if verification is not explicitly forced upon the optimizer as an external rule, an optimizer will inevitably defund its own oversight.

33:21.720 --> 33:24.020
It is mathematically compelled to do so.

33:24.360 --> 33:31.800
Yes, because any dollar spent on verification is interpreted by the algorithm as a strict loss of potential measured value.

33:32.480 --> 33:37.860
Over time, the institution systematically degrades its ability to even see reality.

33:37.860 --> 33:42.460
Wait, my immediate thought is surely an institution would eventually notice.

33:42.740 --> 33:47.200
Like, wouldn't an institution realize its true welfare is dropping?

33:47.360 --> 33:52.760
If they stop auditing, eventually they'll realize they are funding massive failures, right?

33:52.920 --> 33:53.760
You would think so.

33:54.340 --> 33:58.600
But this leads to Proposition 13, the perceived value trap.

33:59.240 --> 33:59.520
Okay.

33:59.680 --> 34:02.620
This is arguably the most chilling mathematical proof in the text.

34:03.160 --> 34:05.200
No, they won't realize it's dropping.

34:05.700 --> 34:06.380
Walk us through it.

34:06.380 --> 34:09.100
Why doesn't the institution realize it's failing?

34:09.660 --> 34:17.460
Because the optimizer only assigns value to the verification department based on the frequency of errors that the verification department is currently exposing.

34:17.840 --> 34:18.480
Oh, wow.

34:18.780 --> 34:19.940
Let me process that.

34:20.000 --> 34:22.280
It's like the smoke detector analogy.

34:22.680 --> 34:23.060
Exactly.

34:23.320 --> 34:23.460
Right.

34:23.620 --> 34:29.940
If you have a smoke detector in your house and it goes off twice a year to warn you about a fire, you value the smoke detector.

34:30.100 --> 34:30.420
Right.

34:30.720 --> 34:33.780
But if you decide to save money on batteries, you take them out.

34:34.140 --> 34:35.780
The smoke detector stops going off.

34:35.780 --> 34:39.660
And the optimization algorithm looks at the data and says, look at this.

34:40.100 --> 34:43.460
Ever since we stopped buying batteries, we haven't had a single fire alarm.

34:44.180 --> 34:47.280
Defunding the batteries was the most optimal decision we ever made.

34:47.620 --> 34:49.920
Our fire rate is mathematically zero.

34:49.920 --> 34:57.240
The algorithm is completely blind to the fact that the house is currently burning down because the mechanism for reporting the fire was defunded.

34:57.600 --> 35:01.620
The optimizer becomes trapped in a self-reinforcing illusion of perfection.

35:01.880 --> 35:03.100
That is terrifying.

35:03.520 --> 35:12.800
Once verification capacity drops below a critical threshold, the institution thinks everything is executing flawlessly because no shortfalls are being registered on the spreadsheet.

35:12.800 --> 35:15.000
Because there's no one left to register them.

35:15.460 --> 35:15.540
Right.

35:15.940 --> 35:20.000
The divergence between the measured return and the realizable return isn't just an accident.

35:20.440 --> 35:23.900
It is actively generated by the optimization process itself.

35:24.180 --> 35:27.200
And it is entirely invisible to the metric that guides it.

35:27.900 --> 35:32.040
The optimization destroys the instruments needed to measure the optimization.

35:32.040 --> 35:34.200
And it feels good while doing it.

35:34.860 --> 35:41.440
We've spent all this time looking at how scalar metrics can accidentally destroy philanthropy and financial institutions.

35:41.720 --> 35:51.300
But Flyxion takes this exact same mathematical structure, all of these trapdoors, and applies them to a massive high-stakes situation unfolding right now.

35:51.540 --> 35:53.100
The governance of artificial intelligence.

35:53.440 --> 35:53.700
Right.

35:54.140 --> 35:57.640
Section 11 of the text is titled, When Loss Becomes Law.

35:57.640 --> 36:01.900
And it is a master class in applying abstract math to immediate policy.

36:02.900 --> 36:08.280
Flyxion analyzes a specific proposal made in 2026 by an OpenAI researcher named Pachocki.

36:09.120 --> 36:17.600
The proposal was to use a scalar metric called perplexity, or predictive loss, as a hard threshold to pause the development of frontier AI models.

36:18.240 --> 36:23.120
Now, for anyone listening who isn't deep in the weeds of machine learning, let's explain what predictive loss actually is.

36:23.140 --> 36:23.320
Sure.

36:23.320 --> 36:26.100
Because it's really the heartbeat of how these models are trained.

36:26.100 --> 36:32.100
At its core, training a large language model is about predicting the next token or the next word in a sequence.

36:32.820 --> 36:36.360
You feed it a massive amount of text, and it plays a game of fill in the blank.

36:36.740 --> 36:40.460
You give it, the cat sat on the, and it has to guess the next word.

36:40.840 --> 36:45.140
The loss is a mathematical measurement of how bad the model is at guessing.

36:45.400 --> 36:45.720
Okay.

36:45.720 --> 36:50.400
A high loss means the model's probability distribution is flat and confused.

36:50.540 --> 36:55.740
It thinks mat, refrigerator, and quantum mechanics are all equally likely next words.

36:56.480 --> 36:59.400
It's essentially a measure of statistical stupidity.

36:59.860 --> 37:00.160
Right.

37:00.720 --> 37:04.680
A low loss means the model is highly confident and highly accurate.

37:05.100 --> 37:08.080
It assigns a near 100% probability to mat.

37:08.080 --> 37:13.700
Over time, as you train the model with more data and more compute, the loss goes down.

37:14.160 --> 37:15.440
The model gets better at predicting.

37:16.300 --> 37:20.660
And Pachocki's 2026 proposal observed a really compelling historical correlation.

37:21.300 --> 37:29.240
As the loss metric dropped on a specific internal OpenAI code base, the model seemed to magically unlock new complex capabilities.

37:29.840 --> 37:35.240
They observed that at a loss of roughly 0.99, models achieved fluent conversation.

37:35.500 --> 37:35.720
Right.

37:35.720 --> 37:40.180
At 0.91, they unlocked productive logical reasoning.

37:41.060 --> 37:49.940
And critically, at around 0.8, they began to exhibit cybersecurity capabilities, coding proficiency, things that start to look dangerous in the wrong hands.

37:50.420 --> 37:52.520
So the governance proposal was brilliantly simple.

37:53.160 --> 37:53.960
Let's use this math.

37:54.380 --> 37:58.080
Let's set a legal or industry-wide threshold based on this loss metric.

37:58.240 --> 38:02.880
If a model is training and its loss hits 0.7, we automatically hit the pause button.

38:02.880 --> 38:07.920
We stop training because the math tells us it's entering a dangerous capability zone.

38:07.920 --> 38:09.400
It sounds incredibly reasonable.

38:09.840 --> 38:11.880
It is a clean, objective, measurable number.

38:12.680 --> 38:21.820
But Flyxion brings the structural hammer down, showing that this AI governance proposal falls through the exact same trapdoors as the effective altruism equations.

38:22.320 --> 38:24.240
Let's look at the first failure point.

38:25.060 --> 38:28.040
The dependence of the ranking on the evaluation distribution.

38:28.040 --> 38:28.520
Yes.

38:28.660 --> 38:35.700
The fatal flaw here is assuming that loss is a universal, objective measure of general intelligence or capability.

38:35.840 --> 38:36.440
Which it isn't.

38:36.520 --> 38:37.020
It is not.

38:37.540 --> 38:42.000
Loss is strictly a measure of predictive performance relative to a specific dataset.

38:42.340 --> 38:45.980
Meaning what you test the AI on completely dictates the score.

38:46.320 --> 38:46.800
Precisely.

38:47.380 --> 38:50.020
Imagine you have two AI models, Model A and Model B.

38:50.520 --> 38:53.340
Model A was heavily trained on mathematics and Python code.

38:53.980 --> 38:57.100
Model B was heavily trained on poetry and historical literature.

38:57.860 --> 38:58.120
Okay.

38:58.600 --> 39:04.340
If my evaluation dataset is mostly code, Model A is going to have a much lower loss.

39:04.820 --> 39:08.100
It's going to look vastly smarter and closer to the danger threshold.

39:08.300 --> 39:14.200
But if you change the evaluation dataset to be mostly prose, suddenly Model B has the lower loss.

39:14.500 --> 39:14.840
Huh.

39:14.840 --> 39:24.020
The mathematical ranking of which AI is safer or more capable can completely invert depending on how you weight the mixture of the testing data.

39:24.020 --> 39:27.780
It is the exact same problem as the thermostat analogy from earlier.

39:27.780 --> 39:31.140
You can't just blindly add my comfort scale to your comfort scale.

39:31.340 --> 39:37.540
You can't just blindly evaluate an AI without deciding what type of intelligence you care about measuring.

39:38.280 --> 39:46.700
A shared governance threshold based on loss requires a shared, universal, mathematically objective definition of the evaluation distribution.

39:47.220 --> 39:48.360
And that does not exist.

39:48.720 --> 39:49.360
No, it doesn't.

39:49.360 --> 39:54.420
The choice of what data to test the AI on is a human, political, subjective choice.

39:54.540 --> 39:56.120
It is not a technical preliminary.

39:56.120 --> 40:10.100
So if a regulatory body decides to test AI models heavily on coding, they might ban a perfectly safe math tutor while greenlighting a model that is terrible at code, but highly persuasive at generating biological weapons instructions.

40:10.660 --> 40:13.920
Simply because biology wasn't weighted heavily in the loss calculation.

40:14.200 --> 40:14.480
Wow.

40:14.480 --> 40:17.900
Which leads directly to the second massive failure point.

40:18.700 --> 40:21.580
The insufficiency of scalar loss.

40:22.420 --> 40:26.120
Average loss does not determine specific hazardous capabilities.

40:26.680 --> 40:29.280
This makes perfect sense when you think about a student's GPA.

40:29.680 --> 40:29.880
Yeah.

40:30.020 --> 40:34.500
If a high school student has a 3.0 GPA, you know their average performance.

40:34.760 --> 40:40.020
But that single scalar number tells you absolutely nothing about the variance of their skills.

40:40.500 --> 40:40.900
Exactly.

40:40.900 --> 40:46.480
A 3.0 GPA could mean the student got a solid B in every single subject across the board.

40:47.180 --> 40:51.620
Or it could mean they got an A plus in advanced chemistry and an F in history.

40:52.020 --> 40:54.220
The average conceals the extremes.

40:54.680 --> 41:03.300
Flyxion shows mathematically that two entirely different AI models can have the exact same scalar loss score, but wildly different capability profiles.

41:03.300 --> 41:12.280
So an AI with a loss of 0.8 could be consistently mediocre at every task imaginable, making it incredibly safe to deploy.

41:12.620 --> 41:21.540
Or it could be terribly stupid at writing poetry and translating languages, but possess an A plus superhuman capability in hacking power grids.

41:21.540 --> 41:24.300
And they both average out to 0.8.

41:24.680 --> 41:33.860
So a rigid regulatory rule that says pause all models at 0.8 loss is mathematically blind to the actual shape of the danger.

41:33.860 --> 41:37.820
It treats a flat line and a massive spike as the exact same thing.

41:38.540 --> 41:42.060
And that leads to the final fatal blow against threshold governance.

41:42.060 --> 41:50.560
If you want a rule that guarantees you catch every dangerous AI before it is deployed, the math requires something practically impossible.

41:51.040 --> 41:53.120
It requires the certification condition.

41:53.520 --> 41:56.540
We are back to the candidate menu from the philanthropic math.

41:56.740 --> 41:57.100
Exactly.

41:57.820 --> 42:06.840
To mathematically guarantee that a threshold is safe, you must have an ironclad mathematical bound on the capabilities of all the models that exist above the threshold.

42:06.840 --> 42:11.620
You have to prove that everything you aren't pausing is definitively safe.

42:12.200 --> 42:16.840
And the paper points out a glaring loophole in the AI space that destroys this guarantee.

42:17.340 --> 42:28.980
The paper explicitly points out that chain of thought reasoning, where you allow the AI to generate hidden scratchpad tokens to think step by step before answering, massively raises an AI's capability.

42:29.340 --> 42:31.300
But here is the critical vulnerability.

42:31.300 --> 42:42.360
Chain of thought reasoning, or giving an AI access to a web browser, or fine-tuning it after base training, does not necessarily lower the base loss metric that the regulators are measuring.

42:42.880 --> 42:47.260
So the model gets vastly smarter, but the regulatory thermometer doesn't move.

42:47.580 --> 42:50.880
So the threshold completely misses a massive route to danger.

42:51.440 --> 42:55.040
These avenues act as the omitted alternatives we discussed earlier.

42:55.400 --> 42:58.540
The metric completely misses a massive route to capability.

42:58.540 --> 43:01.640
It is the ultimate manifestation of Goodhart's law, right?

43:01.940 --> 43:05.020
When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.

43:05.580 --> 43:18.560
If we connect this to the bigger picture, if you optimize your international AI regulations against a single scalar metric like loss, you structurally push every AI developer to find capabilities in the unmeasured margins.

43:18.820 --> 43:24.060
You incentivize the exact same misrepresentation we saw with the contractors submitting the lowest bids.

43:24.060 --> 43:37.300
If the law says you can't have a base loss lower than 0.7, developers will build models that score a safe 0.8 on the test, but use massive chain-of-thought architectures to act like a 0.1 in the real world.

43:37.300 --> 43:46.880
The historical correlation between a metric and an outcome in a lab does not justify using that metric as a rigid governance threshold in an adversarial world.

43:47.220 --> 43:51.140
The math of optimization will actively subvert the intent of the regulation.

43:51.400 --> 43:59.560
So if we take a step back and look at this entire landscape, we've spent this time tearing down the architecture of rational decision-making.

43:59.960 --> 44:00.480
We really have.

44:00.480 --> 44:05.480
We've established that expected value can be hijacked by infinite hypothetical futures.

44:06.000 --> 44:09.800
We've shown that adding up human happiness requires subjective conversion rates.

44:10.340 --> 44:16.480
We've proved that looking at limited menus causes statistical selection bias and actually punishes you for searching too widely.

44:17.000 --> 44:25.840
We've seen how assuming interventions are isolated ignores real-world complementarity, and how relying on observational data blinds us to selection effects.

44:25.840 --> 44:39.480
We've seen how optimizing for lowest costs selects for liars, how optimizing for measured outputs mathematically destroys safety audits, and how using loss metrics to govern AI completely misses the shape and source of actual danger.

44:39.980 --> 44:44.680
If all of this structural math is fundamentally vulnerable, how do we make decisions?

44:45.200 --> 44:46.540
Do we just give up on using numbers?

44:46.960 --> 44:47.160
No.

44:47.520 --> 44:50.140
Flyxion doesn't leave us in a state of mathematical nihilism.

44:50.680 --> 44:52.280
Section 12 outlines the repairs.

44:52.480 --> 44:52.960
Okay, good.

44:52.960 --> 44:58.420
But the crucial, inescapable takeaway is that the repairs do not eliminate the human element.

44:58.880 --> 45:01.040
They do not make the math purely objective.

45:01.480 --> 45:05.200
They simply relocate the human judgment to a more transparent part of the equation.

45:05.440 --> 45:06.640
Let's run through the repairs.

45:06.980 --> 45:08.760
Repair number one, bounded utility.

45:09.080 --> 45:12.980
This is how you fix the speculative benefit domination we talked about with long-termism.

45:13.560 --> 45:19.020
You simply impose a mathematical cap on how much utility any single outcome can generate.

45:19.020 --> 45:24.040
You declare by fiat, no hypothetical future is worth infinite points.

45:24.620 --> 45:28.180
The maximum score any event can have is 1 billion.

45:28.540 --> 45:32.680
It stops the tiny probability from multiplying into an unstoppable force.

45:32.800 --> 45:40.260
But doing that means you, the human, are making a highly subjective ethical choice to limit the mathematical value of future lives.

45:40.420 --> 45:42.220
The math didn't choose the cap.

45:42.340 --> 45:42.740
You did.

45:42.740 --> 45:43.540
Exactly.

45:44.360 --> 45:50.600
Repair number two, robust criteria, like the Maximin rule we discussed with Ellsberg's paradox.

45:51.100 --> 45:52.980
This fixes the ambiguity problem.

45:53.640 --> 45:57.180
You assume the worst case probability for highly speculative events.

45:57.420 --> 46:00.060
If there is no hard evidence, the probability is zero.

46:00.060 --> 46:03.900
But the cost of this repair is widespread conservatism.

46:04.720 --> 46:09.800
It forces you to ignore potential catastrophic tail risks if the evidence is currently thin.

46:10.620 --> 46:13.400
It paralyzes the algorithm in the face of deep uncertainty.

46:14.100 --> 46:18.540
Again, a human epistemic choice, not a mathematical absolute.

46:19.580 --> 46:21.960
Repair number three, side constraints.

46:22.440 --> 46:24.820
This fixes the FTX institutional blind spot.

46:24.820 --> 46:30.520
You introduce rigid, non-negotiable rules that sit completely outside the optimization objective.

46:31.360 --> 46:35.380
Maximize portfolio value, but do not touch client deposits.

46:35.500 --> 46:35.840
Exactly.

46:36.380 --> 46:41.980
Or maximize charitable impact, but mandate that 20% of the budget goes to independent audits.

46:42.720 --> 46:44.920
The objective function didn't generate those rules.

46:45.100 --> 46:47.220
The math didn't ask for a compliance department.

46:47.540 --> 46:49.660
You forced it onto the math from the outside.

46:50.000 --> 46:52.740
And that is the ultimate conclusion of Flyxion's deep dive.

46:52.740 --> 46:59.280
The quantitative apparatus, taken by itself, leaves everything that actually matters undetermined.

47:00.020 --> 47:05.180
The candidate set, the interaction models, the causal inferences, the aggregation scales.

47:05.620 --> 47:09.720
These are the critical junctures where arithmetic must give way to judgment.

47:10.540 --> 47:16.200
To stand behind a spreadsheet and claim that you are just following the math or letting the data decide is an illusion.

47:16.780 --> 47:20.440
The math is a phenomenally powerful engine, but it does not have a steering wheel.

47:20.440 --> 47:25.380
The bounds, the constraints, the verification protocols, those are the steering wheel.

47:25.880 --> 47:28.960
And you have to look in the mirror and admit that you are the one driving.

47:29.340 --> 47:35.820
And if you don't grab the wheel, the optimization process will just accelerate into the nearest statistical trapdoor.

47:36.060 --> 47:36.560
It really will.

47:38.000 --> 47:41.480
Which brings me to a final thought I want to leave you with today.

47:41.480 --> 47:49.400
We spend so much of our modern lives looking at how data and algorithms underdetermine what is good or safe.

47:49.660 --> 48:00.000
We assume we use these intense scalar metrics in our charities, our tech companies, our government policies, because the math is objective and human beings are biased.

48:00.380 --> 48:02.820
We think we are elevating our decision-making.

48:02.820 --> 48:08.520
But think about the crushing cognitive load of true moral responsibility.

48:09.160 --> 48:17.640
When we rely so heavily on data, algorithms, and spreadsheets to run our world, are we truly doing it because the math is smarter than us?

48:17.640 --> 48:28.580
Or are we just outsourcing our morality to the math simply to avoid the profound, agonizing discomfort of having to make subjective, human, ethical choices ourselves?

48:29.200 --> 48:36.020
Are we just typing the destination into the GPS, closing our eyes, and praying we don't end up driving straight into the lake?

48:36.360 --> 48:40.600
It's the ultimate abdication of responsibility disguised as rationality.

48:41.080 --> 48:46.420
And it's the question every institutional leader has to answer the next time they look at an optimized spreadsheet.

48:46.420 --> 48:49.080
Thank you for joining us on this deep dive.

